	# --- scratch-map, the travel map, added 2026-08-28 ------------------------
	# `handle`, NOT `handle_path` -- same reasoning as /logs above. The NUC runs
	# a local fork with BASE_PATH=/scratch (nuc-homelab/scratch-map/docs/FORK.md),
	# so the app generates /scratch/... links itself and the prefix must survive
	# the proxy rather than being stripped off it.
	#
	# WHY A FORK RATHER THAN A PORT. Upstream hardcodes root-absolute assets and
	# has no base-path setting -- the same wall DocuSeal and Uptime Kuma hit, and
	# for those two the answer was a dedicated port. This app is ~500 lines of
	# Express and Pug, so patching it was cheaper than spending a router port,
	# and Jason asked for proxied paths over new ports.
	#
	# THE SSO GATE IS THE ONLY GATE FROM OUTSIDE. scratch-map has no
	# authentication of its own -- none. Its port is on the NUC's LAN address
	# (it has to be: Caddy runs on the other machine), so anyone already inside
	# the house can reach 192.168.1.86:8093 directly and bypass this. That is
	# true of uptime-kuma, dozzle and gitea too and is a property of the LAN,
	# not of this app. What this block protects is the internet-facing side.
	# Without forward_auth here, the family's travel history is public.
	# --- /connect: link your Google Photos, self-service ---------------------
	# gconnect on the NUC (see nuc-homelab/gconnect/app.py). The embedded
	# sign-in screen is a noVNC websocket -- its own sub-route so the
	# streaming bypasses none of the gate but keeps its upgrade headers.
	redir /connect /connect/ 308
	handle_path /connect/screen/* {
